2.2 Volt-second balance sets the duty
Repeating the two intervals at f_s = 1/T_s, with duty ratio D = t_{\mathrm{on}}/T_s, is pulse-width modulation. At start-up V_o is small: the current rises steeply and falls slowly, so it grows from period to period and charges the output. As V_o rises the two ramps come into balance.
In periodic steady state the inductor current returns to its starting value, so the volt-seconds across L cancel over one period. The recap derives this from periodicity (§7.2) and applies it to the ideal buck (§7.3). The cycle average of the inductor voltage is zero, not its instantaneous value.
D\,(V_{\mathrm{in}}-V_o)+(1-D)\,(-V_o)=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad M(D)=\frac{V_o}{V_{\mathrm{in}}}=D\tag{2.1a}
0.250\cdot(48-12)\ \mathrm{V}+0.750\cdot(-12\ \mathrm{V})=9\ \mathrm{V}-9\ \mathrm{V}=0\tag{2.1b}
With V_o regulated, the duty follows the input. The extremes sit at the ends of the input range:
D_{\min}=\frac{V_o}{V_{\mathrm{in,max}}},\qquad D_{\mathrm{nom}}=\frac{V_o}{V_{\mathrm{in,nom}}},\qquad D_{\max}=\frac{V_o}{V_{\mathrm{in,min}}}\tag{2.2a}
D_{\min}=\frac{12}{60}=0.200,\qquad D_{\mathrm{nom}}=\frac{12}{48}=0.250,\qquad D_{\max}=\frac{12}{30}=0.400\tag{2.2b}
Practical duty limits. A real controller cannot reach every duty between 0 and 1. A minimum on-time sets the low end. The dead time and the bootstrap refresh of the high-side driver set the high end. As a rule of thumb, classic silicon designs stay within about 10–90 %, and fast GaN stages can reach 98–99 %. At 60 V, PMP31349 needs an on-time of D_{\min}T_s = 0.571 µs (ours), far from either limit.

4.2 The ripple-only choice
In CCM the capacitor current is a zero-mean triangle of height \Delta I_L. The capacitor charges while that current is positive, for half a period. The charge is a triangle of base T_s/2 and height \Delta I_L/2, so \Delta Q = \Delta I_L\cdot T_s/8. For an ideal capacitor:
\Delta V_o=\frac{\Delta Q}{C}=\frac{\Delta I_L}{8\,f_s\,C}\quad\Rightarrow\quad C\geq\frac{\Delta I_L}{8\,f_s\,\Delta V_o}\tag{4.1a}
C\geq\frac{5.836\ \mathrm{A}}{8\cdot350\ \mathrm{kHz}\cdot0.12\ \mathrm{V}}=17.37\ \mu\mathrm{F}\tag{4.1b}
Our ripple limit alone asks for 17.37 µF (ours).

4.3 The load step: No!
When the load jumps by \Delta I_{\mathrm{step}}, the inductor current cannot follow at once. Until the loop answers, roughly for 1/(2\pi f_c), the capacitor supplies the deficit. Holding the drop within \Delta V_{\mathrm{step}} at our crossover target needs:
C\geq\frac{\Delta I_{\mathrm{step}}}{2\pi\,f_c\,\Delta V_{\mathrm{step}}}\tag{4.2a}
C\geq\frac{10\ \mathrm{A}}{2\pi\cdot35\ \mathrm{kHz}\cdot0.36\ \mathrm{V}}=126.3\ \mu\mathrm{F}\tag{4.2b}
17.37 µF against 126.3 µF: the ripple-only choice fails the load step by more than a factor of seven. No! The load step, not the ripple, sizes this bank.
Never accept a capacitor because the ripple is small. Steady switching ripple proves nothing about transient droop, about the effective capacitance under bias, about RMS heating or about high-frequency behaviour. A load step removes charge in proportion to the integral of the current deficit, and the loop decides how long that deficit lasts.

4.4 Real parts: DC bias, then ESR — No! again
We take 126.3 µF as the minimum effective capacitance. TI uses four 22 µF 25 V X7R ceramics and two 150 µF 16 V tantalum polymers (TIDMDL6). By our DC-bias estimate, each ceramic keeps about 10 µF at 12 V (ours). The bank therefore gives ≈340 µF and passes (4.2b).
A capacitor helps the loop only while it still looks capacitive at crossover. Its ESR must therefore stay below its reactance at f_c:
r_C\leq|Z_C(f_c)|=\frac{1}{2\pi\,f_c\,C}\tag{4.3a}
|Z_C(f_c)|=\frac{1}{2\pi\cdot35\ \mathrm{kHz}\cdot340\ \mu\mathrm{F}}=13.37\ \mathrm{m\Omega}\ &lt;\ r_C=32.5\ \mathrm{m\Omega}\tag{4.3b}
Kemet rates the polymer at 65 mΩ ESR maximum per part (T598D157M016ATE065). Two in parallel give 32.5 mΩ, more than twice the 13.37 mΩ budget of the whole 340 µF. No!
Which branch sets the impedance where? At f_c the ceramic branch still shows |Z| ≈ 114 mΩ, so it does not help there. The polymer ESR sets the impedance of the bank. Its zero lies at
f_{z,\mathrm{ESR}}=\frac{1}{2\pi\,r_C\,C_{\mathrm{pol}}}\tag{4.4a}
f_{z,\mathrm{ESR}}=\frac{1}{2\pi\cdot32.5\ \mathrm{m\Omega}\cdot300\ \mu\mathrm{F}}=16.3\ \mathrm{kHz}\tag{4.4b}
below our 35 kHz target. At crossover the output stage therefore looks resistive, and the compensation must account for it (§7). The ceramics are useful near f_s. At 350 kHz their branch shows |Z| ≈ 11.4 mΩ, lower than the polymer ESR. They therefore carry most of the ripple current and bypass the ESL of the polymers. Without them the ESR term alone would be
\Delta V_{o,\mathrm{ESR}}\simeq\Delta I_L\,r_C\tag{4.5a}
\Delta V_{o,\mathrm{ESR}}\simeq5.836\ \mathrm{A}\cdot32.5\ \mathrm{m\Omega}\approx190\ \mathrm{mV}\tag{4.5b}
That is 190 mV (ours), above our 120 mV limit. The ESR also reshapes the ripple and adds a zero to the loop, so never judge a capacitor by the size of its ripple alone.
What a failed ESR check means. Check (4.3a) assumes we want a capacitive bank at f_c. When it fails, two redesigns remain. The first adds low-ESR capacitance until the ESR of the bank drops below 13.37 mΩ. The second keeps the bank, lowers the crossover and compensates the ESR zero.
TI’s board matches the second route: it crossed at about 15 kHz (TI measured, §7). At that crossover (4.2a) asks for ≈290 µF, and TI’s 340 µF passes it (loop-checks.json).
Decision: keep TI’s bank. The bank passes the capacitance check but fails the ESR check, so the compensation must include the 16.3 kHz ESR zero (§7). TI measured 56 mV peak-to-peak output ripple at 48 V and 20 A (TIDT427A fig. 3-5). The 10 → 20 A load step gave a −276 mV dip (fig. 3-7). Both meet our targets, and the bank keeps the model comparable with the board. A different bank changes ripple and ESR, so it needs a new loop calculation.

The stage draws the inductor current from the input while the high side conducts, and nothing while the low side conducts. If the source behind the input filter supplies only the mean D\cdot I_o, the local capacitor supplies the difference (1-D)\cdot I_o during DT_s. The charge it loses is a rectangle, \Delta V = I\cdot t_x/C:
\Delta V_{\mathrm{in}}=\frac{(1-D)\,I_o\,D\,T_s}{C_{\mathrm{in}}}\quad\Rightarrow\quad C_{\mathrm{in}}\geq\frac{D\,(1-D)\,I_o}{f_s\,\Delta V_{\mathrm{in}}}\tag{5.1a}
C_{\mathrm{in}}\geq\frac{0.250\cdot0.750\cdot20\ \mathrm{A}}{350\ \mathrm{kHz}\cdot1.0\ \mathrm{V}}=10.71\ \mu\mathrm{F}\tag{5.1b}
Our input-ripple target asks for 10.71 µF (ours). No inductor smooths this current: the capacitor itself is the filter, which is why the input ripple is volts while the output ripple is millivolts.
The same pulsed current sets the RMS stress. Neglecting the inductor ripple, and with the source supplying only the mean:
I_{C\mathrm{in,rms}}\simeq I_o\sqrt{D\,(1-D)}\tag{5.2a}
I_{C\mathrm{in,rms}}\simeq20\ \mathrm{A}\cdot\sqrt{0.400\cdot0.600}=9.798\ \mathrm{A}\tag{5.2b}
D(1-D) peaks at D = 0.5, and over our range D_{\max} = 0.400 at 30 V lies nearest to it. The output bank carries only the ripple triangle, \Delta I_L/\sqrt{12}. The input bank carries a pulsed current of about half the load. Always size input capacitors for RMS current at the duty nearest 0.5, not at nominal input.
Decision. Our 1.0 V ripple target at 48 V needs 10.71 µF. TI uses seven 4.7 µF, 100 V X7S ceramics (TIDMDL6). Their nominal capacitance is not the value available under bias.
TI’s schematic notes “1.5 µF effective @ 48 Vin” over the bank. We interpret that as a per-part value: 7 × 1.5 µF = 10.5 µF. This is just below our requirement. Ahead of this bank, TI places a 5.6 µH filter inductor and a 47 µF hybrid polymer. A 0.5 Ω resistor damps that filter (TIDMDL2).
TI measured 1.28 V peak to peak at 48 V and 20 A (TIDT427A fig. 3-6). No! This exceeds our 1.0 V target. TI publishes no input-ripple limit, so this is a failure against our target. §9 shows that the measurement implies only ≈8.8 µF effective. We retain ≈10.5 µF in the model and keep this discrepancy visible.
If 1.0 V is mandatory, revise the bank. More effective local capacitance is the first candidate. Nominal microfarads alone are not sufficient under bias. Recheck impedance and RMS current. A relaxed target avoids that redesign only if the system permits it.

6.1 The half-bridge and its dead time
The LMG2100R044 puts both GaN switches and their drivers in one package (SNOSDF9B). Our model uses 4.4 mΩ typical on-resistance, multiplied by 1.4 for the hot device. The low side replaces the diode of the classical buck (Appendix D), so the conduction drop at 20 A is a resistance, not a junction.
The two switches must never conduct together. The LM5148 holds both off for a dead time t_d at each edge, adaptive and 20 ns typical (SNVSC01 §7.5). During t_d the inductor keeps its current flowing. The low-side GaN conducts it in reverse, in the third quadrant, with a drop V_{SD} of about 1.5 V.
SNOSDF9B gives this value as typical at 0.5 A reverse current and publishes no value at 20 A. The drop at 20 A is likely higher, so (6.1b) is probably a low estimate. GaN has no body diode and so no reverse-recovery charge. What remains is this third-quadrant drop, paid at both edges:
P_{\mathrm{dead}}=V_{SD}\,t_d\,f_s\,\left(I_{\mathrm{pk}}+|I_v|\right)\tag{6.1a}
P_{\mathrm{dead}}=1.5\ \mathrm{V}\cdot20\ \mathrm{ns}\cdot350\ \mathrm{kHz}\cdot40\ \mathrm{A}=0.42\ \mathrm{W}\quad(48\ \mathrm{V},\ 20\ \mathrm{A})\tag{6.1b}

8.1 Where the losses come from: the datasheet estimate
The ideal buck of §2.3 is lossless. The losses come from the components, not from the conversion principle. We first estimate every loss from datasheets only: LMG2100R044 (SNOSDF9B), LM5148 (SNVSC01) and Würth 74439370047, with no measured number (ti-pmp31349/scripts/loss_estimate.py). At 48 V and 20 A (ours):
There is no reverse-recovery row: the GaN half-bridge has no body diode (§6.1). The model does not include skin and proximity effects separately. These effects raise the AC resistance of the winding (recap §2.7).
\eta=\frac{P_o}{P_o+\sum P_k}\tag{8.1a}
\eta=\frac{240\ \mathrm{W}}{240\ \mathrm{W}+7.87\ \mathrm{W}}=96.83\ \%\tag{8.1b}
Our datasheet estimate gives 96.83 % at 48 V and 20 A (ours). TI measured 95.5 %.

3.1 The ripple ratio sets L, and the worst case sits in the middle
Integrate the low-side ramp of §2.1 over DT_s and substitute D = 1 - V_{\mathrm{in}}/V_o. The result is equation (B.2a) of Appendix B.2: \Delta I_L = V_{\mathrm{in}}\,D/(L\,f_s) = V_{\mathrm{in}}(1 - V_{\mathrm{in}}/V_o)/(L\,f_s).
At fixed V_o this is a parabola in V_{\mathrm{in}}. It is zero at both V_{\mathrm{in}} = 0 and V_{\mathrm{in}} = V_o. The ripple peaks at V_{\mathrm{in}} = V_o/2, inside the range, not at an end. There D = 0.5:
\Delta I_{L,\max}=\frac{V_o}{4\,L\,f_s}\qquad\mathrm{at}\qquad V_{\mathrm{in}}=\frac{V_o}{2}\tag{3.1a}
\Delta I_{L,\max}=\frac{54\ \mathrm{V}}{4\cdot10\ \mu\mathrm{H}\cdot230\ \mathrm{kHz}}=5.87\ \mathrm{A}\qquad\mathrm{at}\qquad27\ \mathrm{V}\tag{3.1b}
This is the dual of Lesson 1’s shortcut warning. There, V_{\mathrm{in}} swept with V_o fixed and the ripple grew to the range end. Here the parabola turns inside the range. Find the worst case from the equation, not from habit.
State the ripple against the input current at the same input. The input current falls as 1/V_{\mathrm{in}} while the ripple first rises. The same 10 µH gives very different ratios (ours, TI’s 10 µH, design-checks.json):
The ratio exceeds 1 from 24 V to beyond 36 V. A ratio above 1 is not discontinuous conduction. The valley stays positive because the mean is still larger than half the ripple. The lowest full-load valley is 1.10 A at 38.4 V (ours), not at 27 V, because the input current falls faster than the ripple there. Full load therefore stays in CCM over the whole 12–50 V range.
We ask for a ripple of r = 0.30 of the full-load input current at 12 V, where the current is largest. Solving (B.2a) for L:
L=\frac{V_{\mathrm{in,min}}\,D_{\max}}{r\,f_s\,I_{\mathrm{in}}}\tag{3.2a}
L=\frac{12\ \mathrm{V}\cdot0.778}{0.30\cdot230\ \mathrm{kHz}\cdot11.25\ \mathrm{A}}=12.0\ \mu\mathrm{H}\tag{3.2b}
Our inductor is 12.0 µH (ours). Why specify at 12 V and not at 27 V? At 27 V the ripple is largest in amperes, but the peak current is smaller (7.93 A against 13.28 A). The peak and the conduction loss belong to 12 V. Always say at which input the ripple ratio is stated, and against which current.

3.2 Peak and RMS current with TI’s 10 µH
TI chose 10 µH, below our 12.0 µH. At 12 V it ripples by 4.06 A (ours, (B.2b)), a ratio of 0.36 instead of 0.30. Peak and valley follow:
I_{\mathrm{pk}}=I_{\mathrm{in}}+\frac{\Delta I_L}{2},\qquad I_v=I_{\mathrm{in}}-\frac{\Delta I_L}{2}\tag{3.3a}
I_{\mathrm{pk}}=11.25+\frac{4.06}{2}=13.28\ \mathrm{A},\qquad I_v=11.25-\frac{4.06}{2}=9.22\ \mathrm{A}\tag{3.3b}
The RMS current is 11.31 A (ours, (B.6b)), only 0.5 % above the 11.25 A mean. At this ripple ratio the DC term dominates every conduction loss, as in the buck. Which current measure enters a loss depends on the element. A constant voltage takes the mean, a resistance takes the RMS (Lesson 1 §3.2). Never use the mean current for a resistive loss.

4.4 The load step, the RHP zero and the ESR: No!
When the load jumps by \Delta I_{\mathrm{step}}, the inductor current cannot follow at once. In a boost it is worse: to send more current to the output, the controller must first raise D, and a larger D means that Q1 conducts for a shorter time. For a few cycles the output gets less current, not more. This is the right-half-plane zero of §7. It forbids a fast loop. Until the slow loop answers, roughly for 1/(2\pi f_c), the capacitors supply the deficit:
C\geq\frac{\Delta I_{\mathrm{step}}}{2\pi\,f_c\,\Delta V_{\mathrm{step}}}\tag{4.3a}
C\geq\frac{1.25\ \mathrm{A}}{2\pi\cdot4.24\ \mathrm{kHz}\cdot1.0\ \mathrm{V}}=46.9\ \mu\mathrm{F}\tag{4.3b}
The step asks for 46.9 µF (ours), three times the ripple-only 15.7 µF. The load step, not the ripple, sizes this bank, as in the buck. But here the reason is the RHP zero, not our choice of crossover. TI’s bank, 8 µF of ceramics plus C20’s 47 µF, is 55 µF. By (4.3a) it gives 0.85 V at 4.24 kHz (ours), inside our 1.0 V.
ESR at crossover. A capacitor helps the loop only while it still looks capacitive at crossover. Its ESR must stay below its reactance at f_c:
r_C\leq|Z_C(f_c)|=\frac{1}{2\pi\,f_c\,C}\tag{4.4a}
|Z_C(f_c)|=\frac{1}{2\pi\cdot4.24\ \mathrm{kHz}\cdot55\ \mu\mathrm{F}}=0.68\ \Omega\ &lt;\ r_{C20}=0.70\ \Omega\tag{4.4b}
C20’s 0.70 Ω exceeds the 0.68 Ω budget of the whole bank (ours). No!, marginally. Its ESR zero lies at
f_{z,\mathrm{ESR}}=\frac{1}{2\pi\,r_{C20}\,C_{20}}\tag{4.5a}
f_{z,\mathrm{ESR}}=\frac{1}{2\pi\cdot0.7\ \Omega\cdot47\ \mu\mathrm{F}}=4.84\ \mathrm{kHz}\tag{4.5b}
right at our 4.24 kHz target. At crossover the output looks resistive, and the loop of §7 sees that ESR directly. The BOM value is likely the maximum rating of the part. §7.2 shows that this one input explains most of the loop gap.
Never accept a capacitor because the ripple is small. Steady ripple proves nothing about the step, about the bias, about RMS heating or about the impedance at crossover. In a boost, check the RMS current too: C5–C8 carry the 4.68 A RMS pulse of §2.3 (ours).
Decision: keep TI’s two-stage bank. The benefit: four small ceramics take the pulse at node A, and L2 with a cheap aluminium part keeps the ripple at VOUT below our limit. The cost: the ceramic node alone fails our ripple limit (1.06 V by (B.3a), 1.09 V by the harmonic sum). Only the second stage passes it, and C20’s ESR sits at crossover. The credible alternative moves C20 to node A, drops L2 and gives node A at least 16 µF of effective ceramics: eight 1210 parts at 2.0 µF each, four more than TI. It becomes preferable if the board must deliver a clean output at node A, or if C20’s ESR at low temperature puts the loop at risk. A measurement of C20’s impedance at 4–5 kHz and at the lowest ambient would change the choice.

6.1 Three MOSFETs, two dead times, one body diode that matters
Predict first. In Lesson 1 the hard edge was the high-side turn-on of the buck, at the input voltage. Which device turns on hard in the boost, and at which voltage? Which body diode conducts in the dead times?
All three switches are BSC072N08NS5 80 V silicon MOSFETs (TI). Q1 blocks the output voltage, 54 V, so the 80 V rating leaves a factor of 1.48 (ours). The LM5122 drives the gates from its VCC regulator at 7.6 V typical (TI, SNVS954H). That lies between the datasheet’s 6 V and 10 V rows. We interpolate 7.7 mΩ and multiply by 1.3 for the hot device: 10.0 mΩ per device and 5.0 mΩ for the low-side pair (ours, design-checks.json).
Why two low-side devices. The low side carries the full input current for D of the period: 9.92 A RMS at 12 V (§2.3). Its conduction loss is R_{LS}\cdot D\cdot J. Two devices in parallel halve the resistance and spread the heat over two packages. Q1 carries only I_o on average, so one device suffices.
Silicon has a body diode. Unlike Lesson 1’s GaN stage, each MOSFET has a pn body diode. It conducts in the dead time, with a drop of 0.9 V typical at 37 A (TI’s part, BSC072N08NS5 Table 7). We use 0.75 V at about 10 A (ours, read from Diagram 12). It also stores charge. When the diode is forced off while it conducts, it must give back its reverse-recovery charge, 37 nC typical (TI’s part, Table 7).
The LM5122 sets two different dead times. From SNVS954H Fig. 5 at 54 V we read 55 ns from low side off to high side on and 75 ns from high side off to low side on (ours, read from figure). During both, the inductor current flows through Q1’s body diode. The dead-time loss is therefore
P_{\mathrm{dead}}=V_{SD}\,f_s\,\left(t_{dLH}\,I_{\mathrm{pk}}+t_{dHL}\,|I_v|\right)\tag{6.1a}
P_{\mathrm{dead}}=0.75\ \mathrm{V}\cdot230\ \mathrm{kHz}\cdot\left(55\ \mathrm{ns}\cdot(9.65+4.03)\ \mathrm{A}+75\ \mathrm{ns}\cdot9.65\ \mathrm{A}\right)=0.25\ \mathrm{W}\tag{6.1b}
at 12 V and 2.5 A, with the valley 9.65 A and the ripple 4.03 A of our datasheet loss model (ours, loss-estimate-checks.json).
The reverse recovery happens at the low-side turn-on. At the end of the 75 ns dead time, Q1’s body diode still carries the valley current. Q2 and Q3 then turn on against the full output voltage. They must sweep out the diode’s stored charge before the switch node can fall. That charge is drawn from the output at V_o:
P_{rr}=Q_{rr}\,V_o\,f_s\tag{6.2a}
P_{rr}=37\ \mathrm{nC}\cdot54\ \mathrm{V}\cdot230\ \mathrm{kHz}=0.46\ \mathrm{W}\tag{6.2b}
This is a high estimate (ours). Infineon measured the 37 nC at 37 A and 100 A/µs, and the board recovers about 10 A. The diode the slides draw as an ideal switch costs energy at every hard turn-on. A GaN stage, with no body diode, has no such term.

7.1 The LM5122 boost model with TI’s parts
The LM5122 runs peak current mode. Each cycle it compares the sensed inductor current, amplified by A_s = 10 across the 4 mΩ shunt, with the error-amplifier output. It ends the low-side pulse when they meet (TI, SNVS954H). An external slope ramp set by R14 = 30 kΩ keeps the current loop stable at high duty (TI). SNVS954H Sec. 8.1.1 and Table 2 give the small-signal model of the boost. We use it unchanged (ti-pmp21274/scripts/loop_design.py).
The datasheet writes D^{\prime} = V_{\mathrm{in}}/V_o = 1-D. With R = V_o/I_o the modulator has a mid-band gain, a load pole and the right-half-plane zero:
A_M=\frac{R\,(1-D)}{2\,R_s\,A_s},\qquad f_{\mathrm{load}}=\frac{2}{2\pi\,R\,C_{\mathrm{out}}},\qquad f_{RHP}=\frac{R\,(1-D)^2}{2\pi\,L}\tag{7.1a}
A_M=\frac{21.6\ \Omega\cdot0.222}{2\cdot4\ \mathrm{m\Omega}\cdot10}=60,\qquad f_{\mathrm{load}}=268\ \mathrm{Hz},\qquad f_{RHP}=\frac{21.6\ \Omega\cdot0.222^2}{2\pi\cdot10\ \mu\mathrm{H}}=17.0\ \mathrm{kHz}\tag{7.1b}
at 12 V and 2.5 A, with C_{\mathrm{out}} = 55 µF (ours). The RHP zero is the small-signal form of §4.4: a step up in duty first cuts the current to the output. It adds gain like a zero but phase lag like a pole. No compensator can cancel it. The loop must cross well below it.
Why the zero moves with the load and the input. The dip lasts until the inductor current has grown enough to make up for the shorter Q1 interval. A heavy load needs a large current change, and a large L slows it. A low input slows it too, because the inductor sees only V_{\mathrm{in}} while the low side conducts. f_{RHP} therefore falls with a heavier load (R smaller), with a larger L and with a lower input ((1-D) smaller). The worst case of the loop is full load at the lowest input, the same point as the worst case of the conduction loss.
The RHP zero moves out fast as the input rises, with (1-D)^2:
The current loop adds a double pole at f_s/2. Its damping depends on the slope factor K of SNVS954H Eq. 7:
K=\left(1+\frac{L\cdot6\cdot10^9}{V_{\mathrm{in}}\,R_s\,10\,R_{\mathrm{SLOPE}}}\right)(1-D),\qquad Q=\frac{1}{\pi\,(K-0.5)}\tag{7.2a}
K=\left(1+\frac{10\ \mu\mathrm{H}\cdot6\cdot10^9}{12\cdot4\ \mathrm{m\Omega}\cdot10\cdot30\ \mathrm{k\Omega}}\right)\cdot0.222=1.148,\qquad Q=\frac{1}{\pi\cdot0.648}=0.491\tag{7.2b}
K is 1.15 at 12 V and 1.81 at 48 V, above the 0.5 minimum at every input (ours). With Q below 0.5 the double pole is well damped.
The error amplifier is a voltage type, 80 dB and 3 MHz (TI’s controller, SNVS954H). The network R17 + C18 in series, C17 in parallel, sits from COMP to FB. R19 + R20 = 86.6 kΩ form the upper feedback resistor. Its mid-band gain, zero and pole are
A_{FB}=\frac{R_{17}}{R_{19}+R_{20}},\qquad f_z=\frac{1}{2\pi\,R_{17}\,C_{18}},\qquad f_p=\frac{1}{2\pi\,R_{17}\,C_{17}}\tag{7.3a}
A_{FB}=\frac{20.5\ \mathrm{k\Omega}}{86.6\ \mathrm{k\Omega}}=0.237,\qquad f_z=\frac{1}{2\pi\cdot20.5\ \mathrm{k\Omega}\cdot15\ \mathrm{nF}}=518\ \mathrm{Hz},\qquad f_p=\frac{1}{2\pi\cdot20.5\ \mathrm{k\Omega}\cdot1.5\ \mathrm{nF}}=5.18\ \mathrm{kHz}\tag{7.3b}
(ours, from TI’s parts). With C18 in series the exact pole is 5.69 kHz (ours). The output bank adds C20’s ESR zero at 4.84 kHz (§4.4) and an ESR pole at 33.3 kHz (ours). The loop gain is T = G_c\,G_{vc}, with G_{vc} = g\,(1-s/\omega_{RHP})\,F_h(s)\,Z_{\mathrm{out}}(s) and g = (1-D)/(R_s A_s). We evaluate it with TI’s parts and the BOM’s 0.7 Ω, untuned:
|T(j2\pi f_c)|=1,\qquad \varphi_m=180^\circ+\angle T(j2\pi f_c)\tag{7.4a}
f_c=3.53\ \mathrm{kHz},\qquad \varphi_m=68.6^\circ,\qquad \mathrm{GM}=11.5\ \mathrm{dB}\qquad(12\ \mathrm{V},\ 2.5\ \mathrm{A})\tag{7.4b}
At 12 V and 2.5 A our model crosses at 3.53 kHz, below the 4.24 kHz target, with 68.6° of phase margin and 11.5 dB of gain margin (ours, loop-checks.json).

8.1 Where the losses come from: the datasheet estimate
The ideal boost of §2.3 is lossless. The losses come from the components, not from the conversion principle. We first estimate every loss from datasheets only: BSC072N08NS5 (Infineon), LM5122 (SNVS954H) and Coilcraft Doc 644-1, with no measured number (ti-pmp21274/scripts/loss_estimate.py). At 12 V and 2.5 A, the worst case (ours):
(ours, loss-estimate-checks.json, table_full_load). The largest term is the turn-on overlap. During the low side’s turn-on, the drain voltage falls from V_o while the current rises to the valley:
P_{\mathrm{ov}}=t_{\mathrm{ov}}\,V_o\,I_v\,f_s\tag{8.1a}
P_{\mathrm{ov}}=12.6\ \mathrm{ns}\cdot54\ \mathrm{V}\cdot9.65\ \mathrm{A}\cdot230\ \mathrm{kHz}=1.51\ \mathrm{W}\tag{8.1b}
The overlap time 12.6 ns is half the 25.2 ns switching time that the LM5122’s low-side driver needs to move the 9 nC switching charge of BSC072N08NS5 at 7.6 V (ours, datasheet values). The output-charge term counts what the hard turn-on throws away. The low side charges Q1’s C_{\mathrm{oss}} from the output and dumps the energy stored in its own two devices:
P_{\mathrm{oss}}=\left(Q_{\mathrm{oss}}\,V_o+E_{\mathrm{oss}}\right)f_s\tag{8.2a}
P_{\mathrm{oss}}=\left(32.6\ \mathrm{nC}\cdot54\ \mathrm{V}+0.59\ \mu\mathrm{J}\right)\cdot230\ \mathrm{kHz}=0.54\ \mathrm{W}\tag{8.2b}
\eta=\frac{P_o}{P_o+\sum P_k}\tag{8.3a}
\eta=\frac{135\ \mathrm{W}}{135\ \mathrm{W}+5.19\ \mathrm{W}}=96.30\ \%\tag{8.3b}
Our datasheet estimate gives 96.30 % at 12 V and 2.5 A (ours). At TI’s measured point, 12.039 V and 2.522 A, it gives 96.31 % and 5.20 W of loss, against TI’s 95.34 % and 6.65 W (TI, TIDUDX6 Sec. 4.2). Our estimate misses 1.45 W at full load.
At higher input every current-driven term falls (ours, table_full_load):
“Conduction” sums the two switches, the DCR and the shunt. “Switching at V_o” sums output charge, overlap, reverse recovery, snubber and dead time. The rest is gate drive, bias, core and ESR. The gate drive grows with the input, from 0.15 W at 12 V to 0.58 W at 48 V (ours), because the LM5122 takes the gate charge from V_{\mathrm{in}} through its linear VCC regulator.
The full datasheet breakdown at the four inputs shows which terms follow the current and which stay fixed (ours, loss-estimate-checks.json):
The capacitor ESR terms, below 0.03 W, are left out of the rows. Three terms do not change at all: output charge, reverse recovery and snubber, 1.32 W together at every input. They depend on V_o and f_s only, as long as the low side turns on hard. The core loss follows the ripple of §3.1 and peaks in the middle of the range. The overlap rises again at 48 V because the valley current rises there (§3.1).
Why the boost does not reach the buck’s efficiency. I said it in the lecture of 2026-10-30: the boost looks like the dual of the buck, but its efficiency is worse, and the duality cannot explain that. The numbers do. At 12 V the low side carries I_{\mathrm{in}} = 11.68 A, 4.7 times the output current, for D = 0.781 of the period. Every turn-on happens at V_o = 54 V, the higher of the two voltages. Conduction costs 1.68 W and switching at V_o costs 3.08 W of the 5.19 W (ours). Both grow with the step-up ratio. For a given output, I_{\mathrm{in}} = I_o/(1-D) grows with the ratio, so the conduction loss grows with its square. The switching loss grows with V_o times the valley current, which grows with I_{\mathrm{in}}. Appendix C.2 shows where this ends for the slide example: the gain peaks and the efficiency collapses.

8.2 The losses change the duty
Replace each switch and winding by its resistance and the dead time by its drop. Apply volt-second balance to L1 with the mean inductor current I_L. This is the synchronous counterpart of the slide model of Appendix C.1:
V_{\mathrm{in}}-I_L\,(r_L+r_s+R_{LS})-(t_{dLH}+t_{dHL})\,f_s\,V_{SD}=(1-D)\,\left[V_o+I_L\,(R_{HS}-R_{LS})\right]\tag{8.4a}
D=0.781\ \mathrm{(with\ losses)}\qquad\mathrm{against}\qquad D=1-\frac{12}{54}=0.778\ \mathrm{(ideal)}\tag{8.4b}
(ours, datasheet values, loss-estimate-checks.json). The controller adds duty to cover the drops. The input current rises from the ideal 11.25 A to 11.68 A (ours). TI measured 11.844 A (TI).
Appendix C.1 shows what this does at a high ratio: the series drops grow with I_{\mathrm{in}}^2, and the real gain peaks and collapses. This is a CCM average model. If its valley goes below zero with diode emulation, its assumptions have failed (§10.2).

A.2 Volt-second balance gives the voltage ratio
In steady state the inductor current at the end of a period equals the value at its start, i_L(T_s) = i_L(0). The mean inductor voltage is therefore zero. Weight each interval voltage with its duration:
V_{\mathrm{in}}D+(V_{\mathrm{in}}-V_o)(1-D)=0\tag{A.1a}
10\ \mathrm{V}\cdot0.5+(10\ \mathrm{V}-20\ \mathrm{V})\cdot0.5=5\ \mathrm{V}-5\ \mathrm{V}=0\tag{A.1b}
Expand as the slide does. The terms in V_{\mathrm{in}}D cancel:
V_{\mathrm{in}}D+V_{\mathrm{in}}-V_o-V_{\mathrm{in}}D+V_oD=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad V_o(1-D)=V_{\mathrm{in}}
Solve for the ratio:
M(D)=\frac{V_o}{V_{\mathrm{in}}}=\frac{1}{1-D}\tag{A.2a}
M(0.5)=\frac{1}{1-0.5}=2,\qquad V_o=2\cdot10\ \mathrm{V}=20\ \mathrm{V}\tag{A.2b}
The M(D) plot of the slide. The curve starts at M = 1 for D = 0: Q never conducts, the diode passes the input through, and V_o = V_{\mathrm{in}}. It reaches 2 at D = 0.5, 4 at D = 0.75, 5 at D = 0.8 and 10 at D = 0.9. It goes to infinity as D \to 1. The slope is dM/dD = 1/(1-D)^2 = M^2, so the same duty step moves M much more at high ratios. Compare the buck: its M = D is a straight line between 0 and 1.
The unlimited ratio near D = 1 exists only in the ideal model. Appendix C adds the losses, and T1 finds where the real ratio peaks.

B.2 Inductor ripple
Interval 1 gives the ripple directly. Interval 2 gives the same value, because V_o(1-D) = V_{\mathrm{in}}:
\Delta I_L=\frac{V_{\mathrm{in}}D}{Lf_s}=\frac{V_oD(1-D)}{Lf_s}\tag{B.2a}
\Delta I_L=\frac{12\ \mathrm{V}\cdot0.7778}{10\ \mu\mathrm{H}\cdot230\ \mathrm{kHz}}=4.058\ \mathrm{A},\qquad I_{\mathrm{pk}}=13.28\ \mathrm{A},\qquad I_{\mathrm{valley}}=9.221\ \mathrm{A}\tag{B.2b}

B.3 Output ripple from the capacitor charge
During interval 1 the diode blocks. The capacitor supplies the load current alone and loses the charge \Delta Q = I_oDT_s. This is the pulsed-current case of Lesson 1, Appendix D.5:
\Delta V_o=\frac{|\Delta Q|}{C}=\frac{I_oD}{f_sC}\tag{B.3a}
\Delta V_o=\frac{2.5\ \mathrm{A}\cdot0.7778}{230\ \mathrm{kHz}\cdot20\ \mu\mathrm{F}}=\frac{8.454\ \mu\mathrm{C}}{20\ \mu\mathrm{F}}=0.4227\ \mathrm{V}\tag{B.3b}
The capacitor current also jumps at each diode edge, from -I_o to I_{\mathrm{pk}} - I_o. Any ESR turns this jump into a voltage step. The boost output ripple is therefore larger and more angular than a buck output ripple with the same capacitor. §4 applies this to TI’s bank.

B.4 Power balance
P_o=V_oI_o,\qquad P_{\mathrm{in}}=V_{\mathrm{in}}\,I_{\mathrm{in}}=P_o,\qquad P_{\mathrm{loss}}=0\tag{B.4a}
P_o=54\ \mathrm{V}\cdot2.5\ \mathrm{A}=135\ \mathrm{W},\qquad P_{\mathrm{in}}=12\ \mathrm{V}\cdot11.25\ \mathrm{A}=135\ \mathrm{W}\tag{B.4b}
The ideal boost is a lossless DC transformer with ratio 1/(1-D). It raises the voltage and lowers the mean current by the same factor.

B.5 RMS currents: derivation
The slide gives three RMS formulas. We derive them from the waveforms with two rules of Lesson 1, Appendix D.4.

A zero interval. If a waveform is kept for the fraction D of the period and is zero elsewhere, its mean square is multiplied by D.
DC plus a zero-mean triangle. The mean square is the sum of the two mean squares.

The triangle. On each straight segment of duration \tau, the deviation from the mean runs linearly from -\Delta I_L/2 to +\Delta I_L/2. Its mean square is the same for the rising and the falling segment:
\frac{1}{\tau}\int_0^{\tau}\left[\Delta I_L\left(\frac{t}{\tau}-\frac{1}{2}\right)\right]^2dt=\frac{\Delta I_L^2}{12}\tag{B.5a}
The inductor. The inductor current is I_{\mathrm{in}} plus this triangle. Call its mean square J:
J=I_{L,\mathrm{rms}}^2=I_{\mathrm{in}}^2+\frac{\Delta I_L^2}{12}\tag{B.6a}
J=11.25^2+\frac{4.058^2}{12}=127.93\ \mathrm{A}^2,\qquad I_{L,\mathrm{rms}}=11.31\ \mathrm{A}\tag{B.6b}
The transistor. It carries the rising segment for the fraction D. Each segment has mean I_{\mathrm{in}} and the full ripple, so rule 1 applies to J. Substitute I_{\mathrm{in}} = I_o/(1-D):
I_{Q,\mathrm{rms}}=\sqrt{D\,J}=\sqrt{\left[\left(\frac{I_o}{1-D}\right)^2+\frac{\Delta I_L^2}{12}\right]D}\approx I_o\frac{\sqrt{D}}{1-D}\tag{B.7a}
I_{Q,\mathrm{rms}}=\sqrt{0.7778\cdot127.93}=9.975\ \mathrm{A}\qquad(\mathrm{small\ ripple}:\ 9.922\ \mathrm{A})\tag{B.7b}
The diode. It carries the falling segment for 1-D. Use (1-D)\,I_{\mathrm{in}}^2 = I_o^2/(1-D):
I_{D,\mathrm{rms}}=\sqrt{(1-D)\,J}=\sqrt{\frac{I_o^2}{1-D}+\frac{\Delta I_L^2}{12}(1-D)}\approx\frac{I_o}{\sqrt{1-D}}\tag{B.8a}
I_{D,\mathrm{rms}}=\sqrt{0.2222\cdot127.93}=5.332\ \mathrm{A}\qquad(\mathrm{small\ ripple}:\ 5.303\ \mathrm{A})\tag{B.8b}
The capacitor. The capacitor current is the diode current minus its own mean, i_C = i_D - I_o. Removing the mean removes its square from the mean square:
I_{C,\mathrm{rms}}=\sqrt{I_{D,\mathrm{rms}}^2-I_o^2}=\sqrt{\frac{I_o^2D}{1-D}+\frac{\Delta I_L^2}{12}(1-D)}\approx I_o\sqrt{\frac{D}{1-D}}\tag{B.9a}
I_{C,\mathrm{rms}}=\sqrt{5.332^2-2.5^2}=4.710\ \mathrm{A}\qquad(\mathrm{small\ ripple}:\ 4.677\ \mathrm{A})\tag{B.9b}
Check against the slide. The three derived forms are exactly the expressions of slide 6, which lists them in the order transistor, diode, capacitor. teaching_examples.py confirms the identity over a grid of duties and ripples. It also integrates the sampled waveforms of this operating point numerically and gets the same three values to five significant digits. The small-ripple approximations of the slide are low by 0.54 % for Q and D and by 0.69 % for C here.
What the numbers say. The output capacitor carries 4.710 A RMS for a 2.5 A load. In a buck, the output capacitor carries only the inductor ripple. The boost moves the pulsed current from the input to the output, and its output capacitor must be rated for it.

C.1 The model
Slide 7 adds three conduction losses to the ideal boost: the winding resistance R_L, the MOSFET on-resistance R_{\mathrm{DSon}} and a constant diode forward drop V_D. The model is a CCM model with small ripple: the drops use the mean input current I_{\mathrm{in}} instead of i_L(t). For a resistive load, I_{\mathrm{in}} = I_o/(1-D) = V_o/((1-D)R).
In interval 1 the current flows through R_L and R_{\mathrm{DSon}}. In interval 2 it flows through R_L and the diode:
v_{L1}=V_{\mathrm{in}}-I_{\mathrm{in}}(R_L+R_{\mathrm{DSon}})=V_{\mathrm{in}}-\frac{V_o}{(1-D)R}(R_L+R_{\mathrm{DSon}})\tag{C.1a}
v_{L2}=V_{\mathrm{in}}-I_{\mathrm{in}}R_L-V_D-V_o=V_{\mathrm{in}}-\frac{V_oR_L}{(1-D)R}-V_D-V_o\tag{C.2a}
The volt-second balance is the same as in Appendix A:
v_{L1}D+v_{L2}(1-D)=0\tag{C.3a}
Insert (C.1a) and (C.2a) and collect terms. R_{\mathrm{DSon}} is weighted by D, because Q conducts only in interval 1. R_L is not weighted, because the inductor conducts all the time:
V_{\mathrm{in}}-(1-D)V_D-I_{\mathrm{in}}(R_L+DR_{\mathrm{DSon}})-(1-D)V_o=0\tag{C.4a}
Insert I_{\mathrm{in}} = V_o/((1-D)R) and divide by (1-D)V_{\mathrm{in}}:
M(D)=\frac{V_o}{V_{\mathrm{in}}}=\frac{1}{1-D}\cdot\frac{1-(1-D)V_D/V_{\mathrm{in}}}{1+(R_L+DR_{\mathrm{DSon}})/((1-D)^2R)}\tag{C.5a}
Our derivation gives the expression of slides 7 and 8 exactly. The first factor is the ideal gain. The numerator removes the diode drop for the fraction 1-D. The denominator compares the series resistance with (1-D)^2R, the load as the source sees it through an ideal boost.
The same model as a power balance. Multiply (C.4a) by I_{\mathrm{in}} and use (1-D)I_{\mathrm{in}} = I_o. Each term is a power:
V_{\mathrm{in}}I_{\mathrm{in}}=V_oI_o+R_LI_{\mathrm{in}}^2+DR_{\mathrm{DSon}}I_{\mathrm{in}}^2+V_DI_o\tag{C.6a}
The efficiency of the model follows directly:
\eta=\frac{V_oI_o}{V_{\mathrm{in}}I_{\mathrm{in}}}=M(1-D)\tag{C.7a}
teaching_examples.py solves (C.3a) and (C.6a) numerically for V_o at 100 duties and gets (C.5a) each time.

D.1 Critical conduction: the boundary
Reduce the load current and the inductor valley falls. At a critical point the valley just reaches zero. Below that load, the diode blocks the negative current and the current “gaps” (German: lücken). The boundary is:
\frac{\Delta I_L}{2}=I_{\mathrm{in}}\tag{D.1a}
Insert \Delta I_L = V_oD(1-D)/(Lf_s) and I_{\mathrm{in}} = V_o/((1-D)R_{\mathrm{crit}}):
\frac{V_oD(1-D)}{2Lf_s}=\frac{V_o}{(1-D)R_{\mathrm{crit}}}\quad\Rightarrow\quad\frac{2Lf_s}{R_{\mathrm{crit}}}=D(1-D)^2\tag{D.2a}
Define the load parameter K as for the buck:
K=\frac{2Lf_s}{R},\qquad K_{\mathrm{crit}}(D)=D(1-D)^2\tag{D.3a}
CCM holds for K &gt; K_{\mathrm{crit}}. DCM occurs for K &lt; K_{\mathrm{crit}}. K_{\mathrm{crit}} is zero at D = 0 and at D = 1. Its maximum follows from the derivative:
\frac{dK_{\mathrm{crit}}}{dD}=(1-D)(1-3D)=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad D=\frac{1}{3},\qquad K_{\mathrm{crit,max}}=\frac{1}{3}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^2=\frac{4}{27}\tag{D.4a}
K_{\mathrm{crit,max}}=0.1481\ \mathrm{at}\ D=0.3333\tag{D.4b}
A boost with K &gt; 4/27 stays in CCM at every duty. Compare the buck: its K_{\mathrm{crit}} = 1 - D has its maximum of 1 at D = 0.
Example at the operating point of Appendix B (diode boost, 12 V → 54 V, 10 µH, 230 kHz). At full load, R = 21.6 Ω:
K=\frac{2\cdot10\ \mu\mathrm{H}\cdot230\ \mathrm{kHz}}{21.6\ \Omega}=0.2130&gt;\frac{4}{27}\tag{D.3b}
Full load is CCM at every duty. At D = 0.7778 the boundary is K_{\mathrm{crit}} = 0.03841, that is R_{\mathrm{crit}} = 119.8 Ω or I_{o,\mathrm{crit}} = 0.4509 A. A load current below 0.4509 A takes this diode boost into DCM at 12 V input. The same value follows from (D.1a): I_{o,\mathrm{crit}} = (1-D)\Delta I_L/2.
A synchronous boost in forced PWM, such as PMP21274, does not enter DCM. Its current goes negative instead. §10.1–10.2 apply these relations to it.

3.1 Two switch positions, one average
A switched converter has one set of linear equations for each switch position:
\dot x = A_1 x + B_1 u\ \ \mathrm{(switch\ on)},\qquad \dot x = A_2 x + B_2 u\ \ \mathrm{(switch\ off)}\tag{3.1}
Average over one switching period. The switch is on for the fraction d:
\dot x = \left[dA_1 + (1-d)A_2\right]x + \left[dB_1 + (1-d)B_2\right]u = A(d)\,x + B(d)\,u\tag{3.2}
When A_1 \neq A_2, the duty cycle multiplies a state. The averaged model is then nonlinear, although L and C are linear. The nonlinearity comes from the way the switch network connects them. This is the state-space averaging of Middlebrook and Ćuk (1976). Erickson and Maksimović develop it in chapter 7.

4.2 The boost: the duty cycle reflects the inductor
L\dot{i}_L = V_{\mathrm{in}} - (1-d)\,v_o,\qquad C\dot{v}_o = (1-d)\,i_L - \frac{v_o}{R}\tag{4.3}
Now d multiplies both states: the products d\,v_o and d\,i_L. These two products are the origin of every difference that follows. Write D' = 1 - D. The operating point is
V_o = \frac{V_{\mathrm{in}}}{D'},\qquad I_L = \frac{V_o}{D'R}\tag{4.4}
Linearize:
L\dot{\hat i}_L = -D'\hat v_o + V_o\hat d,\qquad C\dot{\hat v}_o = D'\hat i_L - \frac{\hat v_o}{R} - I_L\hat d\tag{4.5}
Eliminate \hat i_L:
G_{vd}(s) = \frac{V_o}{D'}\cdot\frac{1 - sL/(D'^2R)}{1 + sL/(D'^2R) + s^2LC/D'^2}\tag{4.6}
Read the denominator. It is the LC polynomial with L replaced by L_e = L/D'^2. The switch network reflects the inductor to the output, weighted by the duty cycle. The control engineer’s LC model is right in form, but it uses the wrong inductance. The poles and the zero are at
\omega_0 = \frac{D'}{\sqrt{LC}},\qquad \omega_{z} = \frac{D'^2R}{L}\tag{4.7}
The numerator holds a zero in the right half-plane. Section 4.4 explains it.

4.3 The buck-boost: same poles, a different zero
Take v_o as the magnitude of the inverted output. Then
L\dot{i}_L = dV_{\mathrm{in}} - (1-d)\,v_o,\qquad C\dot{v}_o = (1-d)\,i_L - \frac{v_o}{R}\tag{4.8}
The operating point is V_o = DV_{\mathrm{in}}/D' and I_L = V_o/(D'R). The same steps give
G_{vd}(s) = \frac{V_{\mathrm{in}}}{D'^2}\cdot\frac{1 - sDL/(D'^2R)}{1 + sL/(D'^2R) + s^2LC/D'^2},\qquad \omega_z = \frac{D'^2R}{DL}\tag{4.9}
The low-frequency gain V_{\mathrm{in}}/D'^2 is the slope dV_o/dD of the conversion ratio. Check it by differentiating. With the same L, C and R, the buck-boost has exactly the poles of the boost. Only its zero is higher, by the factor 1/D.

10.2 In CCM the inductor drops out of the operating point
Take the averaged buck (4.1) and set both derivatives to zero:
V_o = DV_{\mathrm{in}},\qquad I_L = I_o = \frac{DV_{\mathrm{in}}}{R}\tag{10.1}
L and C are gone. They set the ripple and the transient, not the operating point. The Lesson 1 teaching buck at D = 0.5 and 2.4 Ω gives 24 V and 10 A for every inductance that keeps it in CCM.
The inductor still stores energy, E_L = Li_L^2/2. In periodic steady state its current is the same at the start and at the end of each period, so the energy that it takes in one period it also returns:
\Delta E_L = \int_0^{T_s} v_L\,i_L\,dt = 0\tag{10.2}
The input supplies the net power. The inductor sets how fast its current can change, not the value at which the current settles.
The impedance argument agrees, with one limit. The 60 µH inductor has zero impedance at DC and 37.7 Ω at 100 kHz, against a 2.4 Ω load. It passes the DC current and blocks the ripple. But impedance belongs to a linear time-invariant network, and the switch network is time-varying. The argument holds only for the averaged model of §3. For an inverter, the filter reactance at the fundamental is small, not zero: the 1 mH of §5 has 0.31 Ω at 50 Hz, against a 50 Ω load.
In CCM the inductor current is a state with memory. Its value at the start of a period is its value at the end of the previous one. A controller can still set it. The duty-to-inductor-current transfer function of the buck is
G_{i_Ld}(s) = \frac{V_{\mathrm{in}}(Cs + 1/R)}{LCs^2 + (L/R)s + 1}\tag{10.3}
and current-mode control is built on it. Without a current loop, the output current follows the output voltage through the load:
G_{i_od}(s) = \frac{V_{\mathrm{in}}/R}{LCs^2 + (L/R)s + 1}\tag{10.4}
